@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-createpatch-no-new-error-type-prefix-suffix-array-diff-cloned-values
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch: no new error type, prefix/suffix array diff, cloned values"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, as a JSON Patch (RFC
+6902), instead of the whole new document. `createPatch(from, to)` is the inverse of `applyPatch`: given
+two JSON documents, it must produce an operation list that turns `from` into `to`, describing only the
+difference rather than replacing the whole document wholesale. RFC 6902 specifies how a patch is
+*applied*, but says nothing about how one is *produced* - there is no canonical "diff" algorithm to
+conform to, unlike dl-001/dl-002's RFC-mandated parsing and application rules.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Add no new error type: like `applyMergePatch` (dl-003), `createPatch` never throws. Diffing is total
+  over all JSON values - any two values can always be expressed as at least a single `replace` at the
+  root - so there is no failure mode to signal.
+- Diff objects member-by-member: a key only in `from` becomes `remove`, a key only in `to` becomes
+  `add`, and a key in both is recursed into (if both sides are still diffable as objects or arrays) or
+  replaced in place. Keys are walked via `Object.keys`, which is already insertion-ordered and
+  therefore deterministic, consistent with the determinism directive.
+- Diff arrays by stripping the common prefix and suffix (the longest runs of deep-equal elements from
+  each end) and handling only the differing middle: if the middle is the same length on both sides,
+  recurse element-by-element (so a single changed element produces one `replace`, not a wholesale
+  rewrite); otherwise remove the old middle (highest index first, so earlier removals don't shift the
+  indices of later ones) and add the new middle. This is exact and minimal for the common cases this
+  feature exists for - an appended/removed/changed element or run - without implementing a general
+  minimal-edit-script (e.g. Myers diff) algorithm, which would detect element moves but add
+  substantial complexity for a case not in scope here.
+- Clone every value embedded in an `add` or `replace` operation with `structuredClone`, the same
+  mechanism `applyPatch` and `applyMergePatch` use for their own immutability guarantees (dl-002,
+  dl-003). Neither `from` nor `to` is read destructively (diffing only reads members, it never writes
+  them, so the `__proto__`-safety concern from dl-002/dl-003 does not apply here), but the returned
+  patch must not alias subtrees of `to`, or a caller mutating `to` afterwards would silently corrupt an
+  already-produced patch.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `createPatch` is not guaranteed to produce a globally minimal patch: it will not detect that an
+  element was moved rather than removed-and-re-added elsewhere in an array. This is an acceptable
+  trade-off for the stated need (avoid resending an unchanged document for a small, localized change),
+  and can be revisited if a client needs move-aware array diffs.
+- Because there is no failure mode, callers cannot distinguish "nothing changed" from any other
+  outcome except by checking that the returned array is empty.
+- `createPatch(from, to)` composed with `applyPatch(from, patch)` round-trips to (a value deep-equal
+  to) `to`; this is exercised directly in the test suite rather than left as an implicit property.
