@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+id: task-005-implement-json-patch-generation-rfc-6902-diff
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch generation (RFC 6902 diff)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `createPatch(from, to)` to `src/index.ts`, producing a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that turns `from`
+into `to` when applied via the existing `applyPatch`:
+
+- Objects are diffed member by member: a key only in `from` becomes `remove`, a key only in `to`
+  becomes `add`, a key in both is recursed into when both sides are still objects or arrays, or
+  replaced in place otherwise.
+- Arrays are diffed by stripping the common leading and trailing runs of deep-equal elements, then
+  either recursing element-by-element into the differing middle (when it keeps the same length) or
+  removing the old middle and adding the new one. A single changed, inserted, or removed element
+  therefore produces one or a few small operations, not a wholesale array replacement.
+- Equal values (checked with the existing `deepEqual` helper) produce no operations at all; two
+  identical documents produce an empty patch.
+- Every value embedded in an `add` or `replace` operation is cloned with `structuredClone`, so the
+  returned patch never aliases a subtree of `to` (or `from`); neither input is mutated.
+- Object member names are escaped into JSON Pointer reference tokens with a new `encodeToken` helper,
+  the inverse of the existing `decodeToken` (escape `~` to `~0` before `/` to `~1`).
+
+No new error type was introduced: diffing is total over all JSON values, so `createPatch` never
+throws (see dl-004 for the rationale, mirroring dl-003's reasoning for `applyMergePatch`). dl-004 also
+records why the array diff is prefix/suffix-based rather than a full minimal-edit-script algorithm,
+and why that trade-off is acceptable here.
+
+Testing followed red-green: added failing tests calling the not-yet-exported `createPatch` first
+(confirmed the import itself failed), then implemented the function until all tests passed. Test
+coverage: no-op on identical documents, a single changed leaf (proving the patch doesn't rewrite the
+whole document), added/removed object members, a changed root primitive, a value changing type,
+appended/removed/changed/inserted array elements, a round-trip through `applyPatch` on a document with
+several simultaneous changes, non-mutation of both inputs, non-aliasing of cloned values, a member
+literally named `__proto__`, and member names containing `/` and `~` (pointer-escaping).
+
+Updated `README.md` with the new `createPatch` section, documented the same way as the existing
+`resolvePointer`/`applyPatch`/`applyMergePatch` entries.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm run typecheck` - passes.
+- `npm test` - 78/78 tests pass (16 new tests added at the end of `src/index.test.ts`).
